1677
01:30:07,569 --> 01:30:08,737
One-on-one, huh?

1678
01:30:09,362 --> 01:30:11,656
<i>The player who draws number one</i>

1679
01:30:11,740 --> 01:30:14,576
<i>will become the first player</i>
<i>for the first round,</i>

1680
01:30:14,659 --> 01:30:16,119
<i>and from subsequent rounds onward,</i>

1681
01:30:16,202 --> 01:30:19,164
<i>the winner of the previous round</i>
<i>will become the first player.</i>

1682
01:30:20,624 --> 01:30:25,003
<i>Once the game begins,</i>
<i>players will have five minutes</i>

1683
01:30:25,086 --> 01:30:29,716
<i>to secretly distribute their number cubes</i>
<i>across their seven cube vessels.</i>

1684
01:30:31,092 --> 01:30:34,679
<i>Each cube vessel can hold only one cube.</i>

1685
01:30:35,347 --> 01:30:36,264
Just one?

1686
01:30:37,265 --> 01:30:40,060
<i>The total sum of all number cubes</i>
<i>placed across the cube vessels</i>

1687
01:30:40,143 --> 01:30:41,978
<i>cannot exceed 30.</i>

1688
01:30:42,729 --> 01:30:46,483
<i>Additionally,</i>
<i>going from left to right in order,</i>

1689
01:30:46,566 --> 01:30:49,402
<i>the number cube in each vessel</i>

1690
01:30:49,486 --> 01:30:52,364
<i>must be equal to or greater than the one</i>
<i>in the vessel immediately before it.</i>

1691
01:30:52,989 --> 01:30:54,407
Equal to or greater?

1692
01:30:54,491 --> 01:30:59,162
<i>The first player will select</i>
<i>one of their cube vessels</i>

1693
01:30:59,245 --> 01:31:01,081
<i>and place it in front of the divider.</i>

1694
01:31:01,873 --> 01:31:04,292
<i>The original position</i>
<i>of the submitted vessel</i>

1695
01:31:04,376 --> 01:31:05,585
<i>will not be revealed,</i>

1696
01:31:05,669 --> 01:31:09,756
<i>and the opposing player</i>
<i>can only see the vessel's color.</i>

1697
01:31:10,590 --> 01:31:16,137
<i>The opposing player will then submit</i>
<i>one of their own cube vessels in turn.</i>

1698
01:31:16,846 --> 01:31:18,682
<i>Once both vessels have been submitted,</i>

1699
01:31:18,765 --> 01:31:21,810
<i>the number cubes inside will be revealed.</i>

1700
01:31:22,435 --> 01:31:24,979
<i>The player with the higher number cube</i>

1701
01:31:25,063 --> 01:31:29,526
<i>will win that round and earn one point.</i>

1702
01:31:29,609 --> 01:31:31,444
It's another point-based game.

1703
01:31:31,528 --> 01:31:35,490
<i>In the event of a tie,</i>
<i>neither player will earn a point.</i>

1704
01:31:36,908 --> 01:31:39,953
<i>Players will use one vessel per round</i>

1705
01:31:40,036 --> 01:31:42,414
<i>for a total of seven rounds.</i>

1706
01:31:43,873 --> 01:31:45,333
<i>At the end of the seventh round,</i>

1707
01:31:45,417 --> 01:31:49,337
<i>the player with the higher total score</i>
<i>will be the winner of the matchup.</i>

1708
01:31:49,421 --> 01:31:53,883
<i>They will then face the remaining player</i>
<i>in another one-on-one matchup.</i>

1709
01:31:54,634 --> 01:31:57,095
<i>The winner of the previous matchup</i>

1710
01:31:57,178 --> 01:32:00,890
<i>will become the first player</i>
<i>for the first round of the next matchup.</i>

1711
01:32:01,808 --> 01:32:05,228
<i>If both players are tied on points,</i>

1712
01:32:05,311 --> 01:32:08,523
<i>a rematch will be held</i>
<i>until the winner is determined.</i>

1713
01:32:09,816 --> 01:32:12,444
<i>Matchups will continue in the same manner,</i>

1714
01:32:12,527 --> 01:32:15,530
<i>and the first player</i>
<i>to achieve two consecutive wins</i>

1715
01:32:15,613 --> 01:32:18,324
<i>will advance to the finals</i>
<i>and go to the living area.</i>

1716
01:32:19,576 --> 01:32:23,163
<i>The remaining two players</i>
<i>will be eliminated from </i>The Devil's Plan.

1717
01:32:23,246 --> 01:32:24,164
Two consecutive wins.

1718
01:32:24,247 --> 01:32:28,001
<i>The game will begin in 30 minutes.</i>

1719
01:32:28,084 --> 01:32:29,627
-It's a mind game.
-Yeah.

1720
01:32:29,711 --> 01:32:30,837
It's all about mind tricks.

1721
01:32:31,337 --> 01:32:33,006
It'll be tough but fun.

1722
01:32:35,508 --> 01:32:37,510
How am I gonna play this?

1723
01:32:38,344 --> 01:32:40,305
-After the first seven rounds,
-Yeah?

1724
01:32:40,388 --> 01:32:45,018
we'll have some leftover cubes.

1725
01:32:45,101 --> 01:32:48,104
-Are the cubes reset?
-The leftovers are discarded.

1726
01:32:48,188 --> 01:32:49,606
-They'll be reset.
-Right?

1727
01:32:49,689 --> 01:32:51,733
So you don't need to worry about them

1728
01:32:51,816 --> 01:32:53,109
-in the next matchup?
-No, you don't.

1729
01:32:53,193 --> 01:32:55,904
I have no idea what a good setup
would even look like.

1730
01:32:57,864 --> 01:33:02,786
<i>You need to keep comparing arrangements</i>
<i>for the most competitive one</i>

1731
01:33:02,869 --> 01:33:06,247
<i>with the 30 cap in mind</i>
<i>throughout the game.</i>

1732
01:33:06,331 --> 01:33:08,374
You need to fake it a bit.

1733
01:33:08,458 --> 01:33:09,793
Pretend to make a mistake

1734
01:33:10,293 --> 01:33:11,920
and act like you're trying to keep calm.

1735
01:33:12,003 --> 01:33:13,671
You know what I mean, right?

1736
01:33:13,755 --> 01:33:15,632
Guys, I just gave you the answer.

1737
01:33:18,510 --> 01:33:21,012
<i>-The prison match will begin shortly.</i>
-Let's go.

1738
01:33:21,846 --> 01:33:23,681
<i>All players in prison,</i>

1739
01:33:23,765 --> 01:33:26,059
<i>-please make your way to the death room.</i>
-We got this.

1740
01:33:26,142 --> 01:33:27,143
Anybody can win.

1741
01:33:27,227 --> 01:33:28,311
-Let's do our best.
-Sure.

1742
01:33:28,394 --> 01:33:29,395
This is so exciting.

1743
01:33:30,021 --> 01:33:31,898
-This is gonna be fun.
-Let's go!

1744
01:33:35,109 --> 01:33:36,361
Hello, dealers.

1745
01:33:37,237 --> 01:33:38,696
It's smaller than I expected.

1746
01:33:39,739 --> 01:33:41,491
-I'll stand here.
-It won't be easy to shuffle.

1747
01:33:42,742 --> 01:33:43,701
Let's go.

1748
01:33:47,038 --> 01:33:51,334
<i>We will now begin</i>
<i>the sixth prison match, Gradation.</i>

1749
01:33:52,085 --> 01:33:54,712
<i>We will first draw lottery balls</i>
<i>to determine the order.</i>

1750
01:33:57,590 --> 01:33:58,716
Hyun Kyung-lyul, you are number three.

1751
01:33:58,800 --> 01:33:59,926
John Park, you are number one.

1752
01:34:00,009 --> 01:34:01,761
JAE JAE, you are number two.

1753
01:34:01,845 --> 01:34:02,971
I'm number two.

1754
01:34:03,054 --> 01:34:05,139
It's over if I lose in my first game.

1755
01:34:06,182 --> 01:34:07,267
Let's have a good final match.

1756
01:34:07,350 --> 01:34:10,436
<i>John Park and JAE JAE, who drew</i>
<i>numbers one and two, respectively,</i>

1757
01:34:10,520 --> 01:34:11,980
<i>will compete in the first matchup.</i>

1758
01:34:12,063 --> 01:34:14,649
<i>The first player is John Park.</i>

1759
01:34:14,732 --> 01:34:16,401
<i>You have five minutes</i>

1760
01:34:16,484 --> 01:34:20,071
<i>to distribute your number cubes</i>
<i>across the seven cube vessels.</i>

1761
01:34:20,154 --> 01:34:20,989
Okay.

1762
01:34:22,156 --> 01:34:26,202
<i>Either you win four right off the bat</i>
<i>with big numbers,</i>

1763
01:34:26,286 --> 01:34:28,830
or go for the long game
by winning by narrow margins.

1764
01:34:28,913 --> 01:34:32,458
It's a true mind game where you have to
keep predicting your opponent's hand

1765
01:34:32,542 --> 01:34:34,460
and beat it by the narrowest margin.

1766
01:34:34,544 --> 01:34:36,504
A one-point difference would be ideal.

1767
01:34:37,088 --> 01:34:40,592
<i>The most important element in Gradation</i>
<i>is the combination of number cubes.</i>

1768
01:34:41,426 --> 01:34:44,137
<i>If your opponent submits a two,</i>

1769
01:34:44,220 --> 01:34:46,848
<i>playing a three</i>
<i>will earn you the same point</i>

1770
01:34:46,931 --> 01:34:49,350
<i>as playing a nine would.</i>

1771
01:34:49,434 --> 01:34:51,978
<i>However, if you use your nine,</i>

1772
01:34:52,061 --> 01:34:54,480
<i>you will lose</i>
<i>one of your most powerful tools</i>

1773
01:34:54,564 --> 01:34:56,900
<i>for countering higher numbers later.</i>

1774
01:34:56,983 --> 01:35:00,778
<i>In other words, responding with a number</i>
<i>just barely higher than your opponent's</i>

1775
01:35:00,862 --> 01:35:04,157
<i>is the surest winning formula.</i>

1776
01:35:04,824 --> 01:35:07,076
We will now collect
the remaining number cubes.

1777
01:35:07,160 --> 01:35:08,036
Okay.

1778
01:35:08,119 --> 01:35:09,245
This is fun.

1779
01:35:09,329 --> 01:35:10,288
Let's do this.

1780
01:35:11,289 --> 01:35:12,665
Round 1 will now begin.

1781
01:35:12,749 --> 01:35:16,044
John Park, please select
and submit one cube vessel.

1782
01:35:21,633 --> 01:35:23,801
If there's a tie,
does the first player stay the same?

1783
01:35:23,885 --> 01:35:24,719
That is correct.

1784
01:35:27,347 --> 01:35:30,767
<i>For starters, I thought it'd be best</i>
<i>not to play too strong or too weak a hand</i>

1785
01:35:30,850 --> 01:35:32,936
<i>right from the beginning.</i>

1786
01:35:33,019 --> 01:35:36,022
<i>So I played something in the middle range</i>
<i>that still carried some punch.</i>

1787
01:35:36,105 --> 01:35:39,067
<i>In other words, I opted for something</i>
<i>that could give me the first point</i>

1788
01:35:39,150 --> 01:35:40,652
<i>with some luck.</i>

1789
01:35:42,570 --> 01:35:43,780
Next, JAE JAE.

1790
01:35:43,863 --> 01:35:46,074
Please select and submit one cube vessel.

1791
01:35:56,751 --> 01:35:59,170
Here is John Park's cube.

1792
01:36:02,382 --> 01:36:03,216
John Park, six.

1793
01:36:03,299 --> 01:36:05,510
Here is JAE JAE's cube.

1794
01:36:07,261 --> 01:36:08,388
JAE JAE, nine.

1795
01:36:08,930 --> 01:36:09,931
What? Nine?

1796
01:36:10,556 --> 01:36:11,432
No way.

1797
01:36:11,516 --> 01:36:13,393
<i>You have to strike first.</i>

1798
01:36:13,476 --> 01:36:17,105
<i>I decided to go big right from the start</i>
<i>to set the right tone.</i>

1799
01:36:17,188 --> 01:36:19,148
JAE JAE wins the round.

1800
01:36:19,232 --> 01:36:20,400
You earn one point.

1801
01:36:20,483 --> 01:36:22,026
Round 2 will now begin.

1802
01:36:22,110 --> 01:36:24,070
The first player is JAE JAE.

1803
01:36:24,153 --> 01:36:27,198
JAE JAE, please select
and submit one cube vessel.

1804
01:36:32,161 --> 01:36:34,247
John Park, please submit one cube vessel.

1805
01:36:45,633 --> 01:36:47,385
Both cube vessels have been submitted.

1806
01:36:47,468 --> 01:36:49,470
Here is JAE JAE's cube.

1807
01:36:51,222 --> 01:36:52,682
JAE JAE, seven.

1808
01:36:53,474 --> 01:36:55,226
Here is John Park's cube.

1809
01:36:56,352 --> 01:36:57,395
John Park, seven.

1810
01:36:57,478 --> 01:36:58,479
It is a tie.

1811
01:36:58,563 --> 01:37:00,732
This is intense.

1812
01:37:00,815 --> 01:37:02,483
No points will be awarded this round.

1813
01:37:02,567 --> 01:37:06,738
<i>I'd be set if I could win several rounds</i>
<i>in a row with high numbers as I planned,</i>

1814
01:37:06,821 --> 01:37:08,364
<i>but my opponent isn't an idiot.</i>

1815
01:37:08,448 --> 01:37:09,741
<i>Once he catches on</i>

1816
01:37:09,824 --> 01:37:11,993
<i>and responds accordingly,</i>
<i>it gets a lot harder.</i>

1817
01:37:12,076 --> 01:37:13,786
<i>It was hard to predict for me too.</i>

1818
01:37:14,370 --> 01:37:15,872
Round 3 will now begin.

1819
01:37:15,955 --> 01:37:18,291
The first player for Round 3 is JAE JAE.

1820
01:37:18,374 --> 01:37:20,168
What happens
if it ends three-to-three in a tie?

1821
01:37:20,251 --> 01:37:21,711
Yeah, is there a tiebreaker?

1822
01:37:21,794 --> 01:37:22,837
There will be a rematch.

1823
01:37:23,880 --> 01:37:24,714
-JAE JAE.
-Yes?

1824
01:37:24,797 --> 01:37:26,966
-Please submit one cube vessel.
-Okay.

1825
01:37:30,803 --> 01:37:32,889
John Park, please submit one cube vessel.

1826
01:37:39,228 --> 01:37:40,855
Both cube vessels have been submitted.

1827
01:37:40,938 --> 01:37:43,066
Here is JAE JAE's cube.

1828
01:37:46,027 --> 01:37:47,153
JAE JAE, seven.

1829
01:37:48,946 --> 01:37:50,573
Here is John Park's cube.

1830
01:37:50,656 --> 01:37:51,616
John Park, eight.

1831
01:37:52,116 --> 01:37:53,951
John Park, you earn one point.

1832
01:37:54,035 --> 01:37:59,040
<i>I felt like giving up a point here</i>
<i>would make the match difficult,</i>

1833
01:37:59,123 --> 01:38:01,209
<i>so I played my strongest card, eight.</i>

1834
01:38:01,292 --> 01:38:03,419
<i>And I knew I'd win that round.</i>

1835
01:38:03,503 --> 01:38:05,463
This really comes down to a single point.

1836
01:38:06,047 --> 01:38:07,465
Round 4 will now begin.

1837
01:38:07,965 --> 01:38:10,343
The first player for Round 4 is John Park.

1838
01:38:10,426 --> 01:38:12,595
John Park, please submit one cube vessel.

1839
01:38:13,805 --> 01:38:16,849
<i>If I had played a black at this point,</i>

1840
01:38:16,933 --> 01:38:20,561
<i>she would've known</i>
<i>I played a zero or a one.</i>

1841
01:38:20,645 --> 01:38:25,066
<i>Then she'd play a one or a two</i>
<i>and easily score a point,</i>

1842
01:38:25,149 --> 01:38:27,068
<i>so I bluffed instead.</i>

1843
01:38:27,151 --> 01:38:28,861
<i>I was telling her,</i>
<i>"I'm playing a gray vessel,</i>

1844
01:38:28,945 --> 01:38:34,992
<i>which can be a one or a two,</i>
<i>so play one of your higher cards."</i>

1845
01:38:44,085 --> 01:38:45,419
Both vessels have been submitted.

1846
01:38:48,548 --> 01:38:50,466
Here is John Park's cube.

1847
01:38:52,385 --> 01:38:53,719
John Park, zero.

1848
01:38:53,803 --> 01:38:56,013
Here is JAE JAE's cube.

1849
01:38:56,097 --> 01:38:57,390
JAE JAE, zero.

1850
01:38:57,473 --> 01:38:58,349
It is a tie.

1851
01:38:58,850 --> 01:39:00,810
No points will be awarded this round.

1852
01:39:01,310 --> 01:39:02,645
Round 5 will now begin.

1853
01:39:02,728 --> 01:39:04,772
The first player for Round 5 is John Park.

1854
01:39:04,856 --> 01:39:07,191
John Park, please submit one cube vessel.

1855
01:39:13,739 --> 01:39:16,409
JAE JAE, please submit one cube vessel.

1856
01:39:21,622 --> 01:39:23,374
Both cube vessels have been submitted.

1857
01:39:24,041 --> 01:39:26,043
Here is John Park's cube.

1858
01:39:28,379 --> 01:39:29,380
John Park, zero.

1859
01:39:29,463 --> 01:39:31,090
Here is JAE JAE's cube.

1860
01:39:32,008 --> 01:39:33,217
JAE JAE, one.

1861
01:39:33,301 --> 01:39:34,677
What? It's a one?

1862
01:39:35,219 --> 01:39:36,971
JAE JAE wins the round.

1863
01:39:37,054 --> 01:39:38,764
You earn one point.

1864
01:39:39,265 --> 01:39:42,268
<i>Predicting your opponent's hand</i>
<i>is really important.</i>

1865
01:39:42,351 --> 01:39:46,606
And the thing is, I was able to work out
all of John's numbers by Round 4.

1866
01:39:46,689 --> 01:39:49,108
<i>Up to that point,</i>
<i>John's highest number was eight,</i>

1867
01:39:49,192 --> 01:39:51,235
<i>then the next highest was seven,</i>

1868
01:39:51,319 --> 01:39:52,737
<i>followed by six.</i>

1869
01:39:52,820 --> 01:39:57,575
Sweet John played them
in descending order.

1870
01:39:57,658 --> 01:40:00,244
<i>The moment it was revealed</i>
<i>that dark gray contained a zero,</i>

1871
01:40:00,328 --> 01:40:02,413
<i>I knew the black in front of it</i>
<i>had to have a zero.</i>

1872
01:40:02,496 --> 01:40:06,417
<i>I had actually set aside</i>
<i>a one specifically to beat his zeros.</i>

1873
01:40:06,500 --> 01:40:09,337
<i>And the moment he played black,</i>
<i>I used that one.</i>

1874
01:40:10,296 --> 01:40:11,714
Round 6 will now begin.

1875
01:40:11,797 --> 01:40:13,257
The first player is JAE JAE.

1876
01:40:13,341 --> 01:40:16,302
JAE JAE, please select
and submit one cube vessel.

1877
01:40:18,763 --> 01:40:20,681
John Park, please submit one cube vessel.

1878
01:40:21,474 --> 01:40:22,516
I see.

1879
01:40:27,521 --> 01:40:28,898
Both cube vessels have been submitted.

1880
01:40:29,482 --> 01:40:31,525
Here is JAE JAE's cube.

1881
01:40:33,277 --> 01:40:34,195
JAE JAE, six.

1882
01:40:35,404 --> 01:40:36,489
John Park, five.

1883
01:40:36,989 --> 01:40:38,407
JAE JAE wins the round.

1884
01:40:38,491 --> 01:40:39,742
You earn one point.

1885
01:40:39,825 --> 01:40:41,202
She's incredible.

1886
01:40:42,495 --> 01:40:44,455
Great strategy.

1887
01:40:45,790 --> 01:40:47,667
<i>Turns out</i>
<i>I've been underestimating myself.</i>

1888
01:40:47,750 --> 01:40:48,793
Me heading to the finals?

1889
01:40:48,876 --> 01:40:50,044
So cool.

1890
01:40:50,127 --> 01:40:51,545
Just imagining it gives me chills.

1891
01:40:51,629 --> 01:40:53,422
Me winning? That's amazing.

1892
01:40:53,506 --> 01:40:54,590
I wanna win.

1893
01:40:54,674 --> 01:40:57,677
<i>JAE JAE has won the matchup.</i>

1894
01:40:58,469 --> 01:41:01,973
<i>JAE JAE will now face Hyun Kyung-lyul.</i>

1895
01:41:02,056 --> 01:41:03,266
Is it over if I lose?

1896
01:41:03,349 --> 01:41:04,225
No way.

1897
01:41:04,308 --> 01:41:06,519
<i>The first player is JAE JAE.</i>

1898
01:41:06,602 --> 01:41:09,855
<i>Please distribute your number cubes</i>
<i>across the cube vessels.</i>

1899
01:41:13,818 --> 01:41:15,278
<i>I racked my brains over strategy.</i>

1900
01:41:15,361 --> 01:41:17,989
<i>Russian Roulette was the only game</i>
<i>I've played with Kyung-lyul,</i>

1901
01:41:18,072 --> 01:41:19,282
